## Why This Is Asked

This question tests triage of a mixed HTTP, MongoDB, Vault path and teaches safe caching of per-request secrets, a common beginner pitfall.

## Key Concepts

- Latency breakdown and instrumentation
- Vault per-request secret calls vs caching
- MongoDB indexing and query patterns
- Safe TTL-based cache and rotation

## Code Example

```javascript
class VaultCache {
  constructor(fetchFn, ttlMs = 60000) {
    this.fetchFn = fetchFn;
    this.ttl = ttlMs;
    this.cache = new Map();
  }
  async get(key) {
    const now = Date.now();
    const cached = this.cache.get(key);
    if (cached && now - cached.ts < this.ttl) return cached.value;
    const value = await this.fetchFn(key);
    this.cache.set(key, { value, ts: now });
    return value;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test cache invalidation after Vault rotation?
- What metrics would you monitor to ensure no stale secrets are used during load?

## Why This Is Asked
Aimed to assess mastery of end-to-end guarantees across HTTP API, DB, Kafka, and Redis, including failure handling and read-model integrity.

## Key Concepts
- Outbox pattern
- Kafka transactional producers
- Idempotent consumers
- Cross-service failure handling
- Read model consistency and testing

## Code Example
```javascript
// PostgreSQL transaction: write user update and outbox row
BEGIN;
UPDATE users SET name = $1 WHERE id = $2;
INSERT INTO outbox (aggregate_type, aggregate_id, event_type, payload, sent)
VALUES ('user', $2, 'UserUpdated', '{id:' + $2 + ',name:' + $1 + '}', false);
COMMIT;
```

```javascript
// Pseudo publisher
async function publishOutbox() {
  const rows = await fetchUnsentOutbox();
  const txn = await kafka.beginTransaction();
  for (const r of rows) {
    await kafka.send({ topic: 'user.events', key: r.aggregate_id, value: r.payload }, txn);
  }
  await markOutboxSent(rows.map(r => r.id));
  await txn.commit();
}
```

